{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1401 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "fde5c14ced411475a70843bd284ac251f4cbca5e9e4bff991b5c17d8cb33db9c" :edb/db-before-t 1400 :edb/db-after-t 1401 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534713 50 #inst "2026-10-08T04:50:04.552Z" 13194139534713 true] [17592186105636 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305016 13194139534713 true] [17592186105636 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305027 13194139534713 true] [17592186105637 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305021 13194139534713 true] [17592186105638 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305009 13194139534713 true] [17592186106290 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534713 true] [17592186106290 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/assets/5e232394bb3f520386068110e42887faa288c31077a660570494ccc5a85b0486.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements is true?" 13194139534713 true] [17592186106290 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1359/Source/Images/q-49005.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y = f(x)$ above, which of the following statements is true?" 13194139534713 false] [17592186106290 1145 "From the graph, we see that\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is zero, so ${f}^{′}(-1)=0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is negative, so ${f}^{′}(0)<0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is positive, so ${f}^{′}(1)>0.$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(1).$" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305009 1017 #edb/ref 17592186305010 13194139534713 true] [17592186305009 1089 "q-132687" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305009 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534713 true] [17592186305009 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/assets/37c80a68fc1ee7dd534c795a7cfae13e44f63b09df4f2e948b29acbd1fb7ff06.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. Which of the following statements is true?" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305009 1113 #uuid "7a3cabfd-8e04-5d9b-aca6-d49b67766ada" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305009 1145 "From the graph, we see that\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-4$ is positive, so ${f}^{′}(-4)>0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-3$ is zero, so ${f}^{′}(-3)=0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is negative, so ${f}^{′}(-1)<0.$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(-3)<{f}^{′}(-4).$" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305010 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305011 13194139534713 true] [17592186305010 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305012 13194139534713 true] [17592186305010 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305013 13194139534713 true] [17592186305010 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305014 13194139534713 true] [17592186305010 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305015 13194139534713 true] [17592186305010 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534713 true] [17592186305010 1041 "selection" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305010 1104 #edb/ref 17592186305013 13194139534713 true] [17592186305010 1110 #uuid "2cf51bee-fb6f-5fed-94ba-81c4cae4cc19" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305011 1034 "{f}^{′}(-3)<{f}^{′}(-4)<{f}^{′}(-1)" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305011 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534713 true] [17592186305011 1111 #uuid "c2f4e51c-127b-53ff-806a-0c84f06270f9" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305012 1034 "{f}^{′}(-4)<{f}^{′}(-3)<{f}^{′}(-1)" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305012 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534713 true] [17592186305012 1111 #uuid "84d98ff2-f561-5fce-9f97-55116b362885" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305013 1034 "{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(-3)<{f}^{′}(-4)" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305013 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534713 true] [17592186305013 1111 #uuid "55b304d2-3ae3-529e-8107-dbea84d7880c" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305014 1034 "{f}^{′}(-3)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(-4)" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305014 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534713 true] [17592186305014 1111 #uuid "b20e16f3-6b33-5faa-b19e-dd59e381b293" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305015 1034 "{f}^{′}(-4)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(-3)" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305015 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534713 true] [17592186305015 1111 #uuid "e275a98c-4665-5001-9112-ee361c3c9c13" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305016 1017 #edb/ref 17592186305017 13194139534713 true] [17592186305016 1017 #edb/ref 17592186305019 13194139534713 true] [17592186305016 1089 "q-316736" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305016 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534713 true] [17592186305016 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/assets/6c3a45ac200d1f65c326354297e6e03b5347b4d4ec3fbb2a99bb7cde98376db8.png)\n\nFind the values of $x$ for which the function $g(x)=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{x}$ (plotted above) is strictly decreasing.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305016 1113 #uuid "a451281a-7cb5-5533-914f-03710ddd18f3" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305016 1145 "The function is strictly decreasing where ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $g(x)$ using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{3x}\\cdot 3)x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{2(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))}{{x}^{2}}<0.\n$$\n\nSince $x>0$ is the domain of $g$ and ${x}^{2}>0$ for $x>0,$ the denominator is positive; thus this reduces to\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)<0.\n$$\n\nSolving $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=1$ gives the critical point\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{e}{3}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, this critical point is a maximum. The slope of the tangent lines is positive to the left of the critical point and negative to the right of the critical point. \n\nSo, we summarize the sign of the derivative in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\begin{aligned}x & (0,\\frac{e}{3}) & \\frac{e}{3} & (\\frac{e}{3},\\infty ) \\\\ g & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ \\\\ {g}^{′} & + & 0 & -\\end{aligned}\\right|\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the function is strictly decreasing on the interval $(\\frac{e}{3},\\infty ).$" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305017 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305018 13194139534713 true] [17592186305017 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534713 true] [17592186305017 1041 "field-1" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305017 1104 #edb/ref 17592186305018 13194139534713 true] [17592186305017 1110 #uuid "5cd94be4-28fc-50b5-82d9-54cc9576b64d" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305018 1034 "\\frac{e}{3}" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305018 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534713 true] [17592186305018 1111 #uuid "6cb9ad38-fab0-5865-8f12-25906774fb82" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305019 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305020 13194139534713 true] [17592186305019 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534713 true] [17592186305019 1041 "field-2" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305019 1104 #edb/ref 17592186305020 13194139534713 true] [17592186305019 1110 #uuid "2b8fa25b-4190-542f-967c-5ecc18656dcc" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305020 1034 "\\infty" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305020 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534713 true] [17592186305020 1111 #uuid "1b8dbaea-f198-5983-ba66-4c61643200f2" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305021 1017 #edb/ref 17592186305022 13194139534713 true] [17592186305021 1017 #edb/ref 17592186305025 13194139534713 true] [17592186305021 1089 "q-316787" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305021 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534713 true] [17592186305021 1100 "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $g(x)=({x}^{2}-3){e}^{x}$ is strictly increasing.\n\n$x<$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,x>$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305021 1113 #uuid "40fd8bf3-679e-5c97-a32d-0db3134faa3c" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305021 1145 "The function is strictly increasing where ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $g(x)$ using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}-3){e}^{x}+2x\\,{e}^{x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x-3){e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-3){e}^{x}>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}>0$ for all real $x,$ this reduces to\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x-3>0.\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x-1)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=(x+3)(x-1)$ has roots at $x=-3$ and $x=1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/assets/9b847390fb6de568144d309c45fb8c904475bdde79a46a90ec0fd4426a71b57b.png)\n\nFor an upward-opening parabola, the product $(x+3)(x-1)$ is positive when $x<-3$ or $x>1.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the intervals \n\n$$\nx<-3\\,\\text{and}\\,x>1.\n$$" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305022 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305023 13194139534713 true] [17592186305022 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305024 13194139534713 true] [17592186305022 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534713 true] [17592186305022 1041 "field-1" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305022 1104 #edb/ref 17592186305023 13194139534713 true] [17592186305022 1110 #uuid "6a681c15-566d-5a7c-a7a5-a235d5ecd1d5" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305023 1034 "-3" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305023 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534713 true] [17592186305023 1111 #uuid "ec45d8d6-8aa8-53f6-bc17-33be34484578" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305024 1034 "-1" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305024 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534713 true] [17592186305024 1111 #uuid "50043f23-68dc-50c7-99ba-4ec2207e390a" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305025 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305026 13194139534713 true] [17592186305025 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534713 true] [17592186305025 1041 "field-2" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305025 1104 #edb/ref 17592186305026 13194139534713 true] [17592186305025 1110 #uuid "92db79fb-3ce2-5590-9b3e-79e6af7b599f" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305026 1034 "1" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305026 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534713 true] [17592186305026 1111 #uuid "4d355f52-cec2-57fc-b0bd-143c40abdc5e" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305027 1017 #edb/ref 17592186305028 13194139534713 true] [17592186305027 1017 #edb/ref 17592186305030 13194139534713 true] [17592186305027 1089 "q-343574" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305027 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534713 true] [17592186305027 1100 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{2}{5}\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}+2$ is defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find the values of $x$ for which the function is strictly decreasing.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305027 1113 #uuid "74254c39-aaac-5f10-9749-e2334224a425" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305027 1145 "We first write $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}-\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3/2}+2.\n$$\n\nThe function is strictly decreasing where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{3/2}-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={x}^{3/2}-2{x}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={x}^{1/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =(x-2)\\sqrt{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(x-2)\\sqrt{x}<0.\n$$\n\nThe expression $\\sqrt{x}$ is always positive. So, this reduces to\n\n$$\nx-2<0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & <0 \\\\ x & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is restricted to $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ we conclude that $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $x\\in (0,2).$" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305028 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305029 13194139534713 true] [17592186305028 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534713 true] [17592186305028 1041 "field-1" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305028 1104 #edb/ref 17592186305029 13194139534713 true] [17592186305028 1110 #uuid "d7604896-01f4-554f-99d0-23623f063e9c" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305029 1034 "0" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305029 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534713 true] [17592186305029 1111 #uuid "47f9a5c4-dfa7-503e-8dae-ec6e794546df" 13194139534713 true] [17592186305030 1000 #ed